 - Kuntz, Thomas, Stan, and Barahona (SIAM Journal on Optimization 2021), "Approximations of Countably Infinite Linear Programs over Bounded Measure Spaces," is verified. It may provide language for countably infinite LP approximation and bounded measure-space feasibility, but any application to the fixed-envelope criterion remains speculative.
 - Lindley (Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society 1952), "The theory of queues with a single server," is verified. It is useful only as background for possible reflected-surplus/debt recursions in two-level drought bookkeeping; it is not a market-making result.
+- Ayer, Brunk, Ewing, Reid, and Silverman (Annals of Mathematical Statistics 1955) is verified as the classical source behind pooling adjacent violators/isotonic estimation. It is relevant proof-technology background for possible pooling rules in unequal-low drought LPs; it does not prove the reduced drought LP or a delayed-market-making theorem.
+- Chakravarti (Mathematics of Operations Research 1989) is verified. It treats isotonic median regression via linear programming and is useful only as an analogy for deriving pooling/PAVA-like algorithms from LP duals.
+- Federgruen and Groenevelt (Operations Research 1986) is verified. It studies greedy optimality for resource-allocation problems with nested/group constraints; relevant as a possible proof-technology analogy for front-loaded low-mass allocation, not direct support for the drought formula.
 
